::ENG3_U06_L05_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation argues that setting affects a character's values.
Which multimedia element best supports that purpose?{
~A decorative slide transition#Incorrect. A transition does not explain the setting or the character's values.
=A labeled image of the setting with a caption tied to the claim#Correct. The image and caption help the audience connect setting to the presentation's claim.
~A sound effect after every bullet point#Incorrect. Sound effects can distract from the evidence and analysis.
~A title font that looks old-fashioned#Incorrect. Font choice may affect style, but it does not give the audience useful evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student includes a photograph but does not explain it.
What is the most useful revision?{
~Make the photograph larger#Incorrect. Size alone does not explain the photograph's purpose.
~Put the photograph on a separate slide with no words#Incorrect. This still leaves the audience without a connection to the claim.
~Add three more photographs from the same source#Incorrect. More media does not automatically improve understanding.
=Add a caption explaining how the photograph clarifies the evidence#Correct. A caption helps the audience understand why the image belongs in the presentation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student needs to explain changes over time in a research presentation.
Which media choice is strongest?{
=A timeline that labels the most important dates#Correct. A timeline helps the audience understand sequence and focus on the most relevant evidence.
~A collage of unrelated pictures#Incorrect. A collage may be visual, but unrelated pictures do not clarify changes over time.
~A background pattern with many colors#Incorrect. A pattern is decorative and does not explain sequence.
~A long paragraph copied onto a slide#Incorrect. A long paragraph may overwhelm the audience instead of improving understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q04::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student says the purpose of a chart is "to make the presentation look smart."
Which response shows better thinking?{
~The chart should stay because charts always impress audiences.#Incorrect. A chart must help explain the content, not simply impress viewers.
~The chart should be replaced with a funny image.#Incorrect. Humor does not necessarily improve understanding.
=The chart should stay only if it helps the audience understand the evidence.#Correct. Multimedia should be included because it clarifies the message for the audience.
~The chart should be made harder to read so it looks advanced.#Incorrect. Difficult visuals weaken audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q05::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide has six images and no explanation.
What is the likely problem?{
~The slide has too little color#Incorrect. The main issue is not color; it is the lack of purpose and explanation.
=The audience may not know which image supports the claim#Correct. Too many unexplained images can confuse the audience and weaken the presentation.
~The slide should include no visuals ever#Incorrect. Visuals can be useful when they support the message.
~The speaker should remove all words from every slide#Incorrect. Clear labels and captions often help audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q06::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student chooses a map for a presentation about a journey in a text.
What caption would best support the audience?{
=This map shows the route that shapes the character's major decisions.#Correct. The caption connects the map to the presentation's analysis.
~This is a map I found online.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the map supports audience understanding.
~Maps are useful in many classes.#Incorrect. This is general and does not connect to the specific presentation.
~Look at this picture closely.#Incorrect. The audience needs a purposeful explanation, not a vague command.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q07::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student wants to use academic vocabulary while explaining a visual.
Which explanation is strongest?{
~This picture is nice and makes the slide better.#Incorrect. This explanation is vague and does not use precise academic language.
~This image is cool because it looks dramatic.#Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of analysis or audience understanding.
~This visual has things that connect to stuff in the story.#Incorrect. The language is too vague for a clear presentation.
=This image clarifies the historical context that influences the character's conflict.#Correct. The explanation uses precise academic vocabulary and connects the visual to analysis.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation includes a source note under an image.
Why is the source note useful?{
~It makes the image look smaller#Incorrect. A source note is not mainly about size or design.
=It shows where the image came from and supports responsible presentation practice#Correct. Source notes help audiences understand and trust the use of multimedia.
~It replaces the need to explain the image#Incorrect. A source note does not replace analysis or explanation.
~It lets the speaker skip the claim#Incorrect. The claim is still necessary for a clear presentation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q09::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student adds background music during a serious explanation.
When would the music be appropriate?{
~When it is loud enough to cover the speaker#Incorrect. Music that covers the speaker harms audience understanding.
=When it is brief, purposeful, and directly supports the audience's understanding#Correct. Multimedia should be controlled and tied to the message.
~When the student likes the song#Incorrect. Personal preference does not make multimedia useful for the audience.
~When the music has no connection to the topic#Incorrect. Unrelated music distracts from the presentation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q10::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student creates a digital presentation for a research claim.
Which question best checks multimedia quality?{
=Does this multimedia help the audience understand my claim or evidence?#Correct. This question focuses on audience understanding and purpose.
~Does this multimedia make my presentation longer?#Incorrect. Length is not a measure of quality.
~Does this multimedia use the brightest color?#Incorrect. Bright color does not guarantee clarity or relevance.
~Does this multimedia replace my explanation?#Incorrect. Multimedia should support explanation, not replace it.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q11::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A graph appears on a slide with tiny labels.
What revision best improves audience experience?{
~Keep the graph tiny so all details fit#Incorrect. Tiny labels make information hard to read.
~Remove the graph and add clip art#Incorrect. Clip art may not support the research.
=Simplify the graph and label only the details connected to the claim#Correct. Simplifying and labeling relevant details helps the audience understand the evidence.
~Add a long paragraph beside the graph#Incorrect. Too much text can overload the slide.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q12::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student says, "My image shows the main idea."
What should the student add?{
~A second image with no caption#Incorrect. More images do not fix an unclear explanation.
~A brighter border#Incorrect. A border does not explain the image's connection to the main idea.
~A sound effect#Incorrect. A sound effect is unlikely to clarify the analytical connection.
=A specific explanation of how the image supports the main idea#Correct. The audience needs to know how the multimedia connects to the claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q13::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide includes a quote and an image.
Which arrangement best supports understanding?{
~Put the quote and image anywhere there is space#Incorrect. Placement should help the audience see the connection.
=Place the image near the quote and explain how both support the point#Correct. Proximity and explanation help the audience connect media, evidence, and analysis.
~Hide the quote in small text below the image#Incorrect. Important evidence should be readable.
~Use the image only and remove the explanation#Incorrect. The speaker still needs to explain the connection to the claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q14::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A presentation uses a video clip.
What is the best reason to include it?{
=The clip demonstrates a key concept that the audience may not understand from text alone.#Correct. Video is useful when it clarifies a concept for the audience.
~The clip is longer than the presentation notes.#Incorrect. Length does not make a clip useful.
~The clip is popular online.#Incorrect. Popularity is not the same as relevance.
~The clip gives the speaker a break from talking.#Incorrect. Multimedia should support communication, not replace responsibility for explanation.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q15::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student is choosing between two images.
Which image is the better choice?{
~The image with the most dramatic colors, even if unrelated#Incorrect. Dramatic color does not matter if the image is unrelated.
~The image that fills the whole slide but has no clear purpose#Incorrect. Size does not create relevance.
~The image the speaker likes most personally#Incorrect. Personal preference is not the main criterion.
=The image that most clearly supports the claim and can be explained in a caption#Correct. Relevance and explainability make the image useful for the audience.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q16::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student includes a table of research findings.
Which change would help the audience most?{
~Add every number from the original source#Incorrect. Too many numbers can overwhelm the audience.
=Highlight the findings that directly support the presentation claim#Correct. Highlighting relevant findings helps the audience focus on the evidence that matters.
~Remove the title and labels#Incorrect. Titles and labels help the audience understand the table.
~Use smaller font so more data fits#Incorrect. Smaller font may make the table unreadable.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q17::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student wants multimedia to improve a presentation.
Which plan is strongest?{
=Use one relevant visual, label it clearly, and explain its connection to the claim.#Correct. This plan uses multimedia purposefully and supports audience understanding.
~Use as many visuals as possible so the audience stays busy.#Incorrect. Too many visuals can distract or overload the audience.
~Use visuals only on the title slide.#Incorrect. Multimedia should appear where it supports understanding.
~Use visuals without labels so the slides look clean.#Incorrect. Missing labels can make multimedia confusing.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q18::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student writes a caption with several spelling errors.
Why should the caption be revised?{
~Captions should be removed from all presentations#Incorrect. Captions are useful when they explain multimedia.
~Spelling only matters in essays#Incorrect. Conventions matter in presentation materials too.
=Errors can distract the audience and weaken the credibility of the presentation#Correct. Correct conventions support clarity and audience trust.
~Errors make the slide more casual and friendly#Incorrect. Errors usually reduce quality and clarity.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q19::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student uses a chart that contradicts the spoken claim.
What should the student do?{
~Keep the chart because visuals are always helpful#Incorrect. A contradictory chart confuses the audience.
~Ignore the contradiction and speak quickly#Incorrect. Avoiding the problem does not improve understanding.
~Make the chart smaller so no one notices#Incorrect. Hiding the issue does not solve the problem.
=Revise the claim or replace the chart so the multimedia accurately supports the message#Correct. Multimedia must align accurately with the presentation's claim and evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q20::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A slide has a strong image but no connection to the evidence.
Which speaker note best fixes the problem?{
~This image is here because I needed another slide.#Incorrect. This shows the image is not purposeful.
=This image helps show the environment described in the evidence, which explains the character's decision.#Correct. This note connects the image to evidence and analysis.
~This image is pretty and makes the presentation better.#Incorrect. This focuses on appearance instead of understanding.
~This image does not need explanation.#Incorrect. Audiences often need explanation to connect multimedia to the claim.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q21::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student is designing slides for an audience unfamiliar with the topic.
Which choice best supports that audience?{
=Include brief labels and visuals that define key ideas before analysis begins.#Correct. An unfamiliar audience needs context before deeper analysis.
~Use unexplained expert terms to sound advanced.#Incorrect. Unexplained terms may confuse the audience.
~Skip background information entirely.#Incorrect. Without background, the audience may not understand the analysis.
~Use only decorative media.#Incorrect. Decoration does not build audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q22::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student uses a diagram in a presentation.
What makes the diagram effective?{
~It includes every detail from the student's notes#Incorrect. Too many details can make a diagram hard to understand.
~It is unrelated but visually interesting#Incorrect. Interest is not enough if the diagram does not support the message.
~It replaces all spoken explanation#Incorrect. A diagram should support explanation, not eliminate it.
=It organizes complex information so the audience can follow the relationship between ideas#Correct. Effective diagrams make relationships easier to understand.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q23::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student is unsure whether to include an audio clip.
Which test should the student use?{
~Does it make the presentation feel longer?#Incorrect. Length does not show quality.
=Can I explain how it improves the audience's understanding of my point?#Correct. If the student can connect the clip to understanding, it may be useful.
~Do I personally like listening to it?#Incorrect. Personal preference is not enough.
~Will it surprise the audience even if unrelated?#Incorrect. Surprise without relevance can distract.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q24::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student has a slide with a quote, a chart, two images, and a video link.
What is the best revision?{
~Add another image so the slide looks balanced#Incorrect. More media may increase overload.
~Remove the quote because visuals are always better#Incorrect. Evidence may be necessary, so removal should not be automatic.
=Choose the multimedia element that best clarifies the point and move extra material elsewhere#Correct. Selecting the most useful media reduces overload and improves audience understanding.
~Keep everything because all information is helpful#Incorrect. Too much information on one slide can confuse the audience.
}

::ENG3_U06_L05_Q25::MLA.ENG3.C.02
Context: A student revises a multimedia presentation before submitting.
Which final check best matches the lesson standard?{
=Each multimedia element improves audience understanding of the claim, evidence, or research.#Correct. This directly checks whether the presentation uses multimedia to improve the audience experience.
~Each slide has at least three colors.#Incorrect. Color count does not measure audience understanding.
~Each slide has the same number of pictures.#Incorrect. Equal picture count does not matter if the media is not purposeful.
~Each image is larger than the text.#Incorrect. Size alone does not make multimedia effective.
}
